The clean-code family — the two skills and one rule file listed below —
adapts the rule-catalog concept and rule codes from Robert C. Martin,
"Clean Code: A Handbook of Agile Software Craftsmanship" (Prentice Hall,
2008), Chapter 17 "Smells and Heuristics" (F.I.R.S.T. from Chapter 9).
Rules are cited by code and short title only; all prose and all code
examples are original work by Lowell Mason, MIT licensed. No Clean Code
text is reproduced and nothing from the book is bundled.

    clean-coder/
    clean-code/
    rules/clean-code-python.md

clean-code/ additionally carries a Modules category (rules M1-M4, detail in
clean-code/references/modules.md) that is NOT from Clean Code. Those four rules
are adapted by Lowell Mason from the `python-module-design` skill in
mancusolab/coding-skills (https://github.com/mancusolab/coding-skills), MIT
licensed, Copyright (c) 2026 Mancuso Lab; the full upstream license text is in
LICENSE-coding-skills and modifications are released under the same MIT terms.
The `M` prefix is unused in Martin's C/E/F/G/J/N/T scheme, so the two rule
families stay distinguishable in citations.

Changes from upstream:
  - Scope cut from eleven rules to four, on measured evidence: a RED baseline
    (specs/red-baseline-module-granularity-2026-08-26.md) showed agents already
    satisfy the dropped rules unprompted, so only the rules they miss were
    ported.
  - Reframed from Codex skill frontmatter into this repo's clean-code catalog
    form (rule-code table plus a references/ detail file).
  - Examples rewritten to this stack (Polars/httpx BLS ETL); no upstream prose
    or code is reproduced.

clean-coder/ additionally draws on, by citation of the ideas only: Kent
Beck, "Tidy First?" (O'Reilly, 2023) — the tidy/behavior commit separation
and the batch-size stopping rule; Martin Fowler's "Opportunistic
Refactoring" — the litter-pickup framing; and John Ousterhout, "A
Philosophy of Software Design" (2nd ed., 2021) — deep modules and
comments-as-design, used to temper the tiny-function and comment-minimalism
readings. No text from any of these is reproduced.

